On Tuesday, May 14, 2024 at 10:08:41 AM UTC-6 Sally paganin wrote:
> Hi Kaitlin,
>
> it seems to me that you may have already seen this conversation
>
> https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/ROO-OMl3hlE/m/ZBXozqiCAAAJ
>
> and that your setting is similar to the selection of covariates with interaction terms, in the sense that the quadratic term can be included in the model only if the linear one is already included.
>
> So to answer your questions:
>
> 1) Both options are acceptable in nimble but the drawback of option A is that the regression coefficient for the quadratic term would be sampled even when it is not included in the model. This happens when z[1] = 0 and z[2] = 1. The RJ sampler with indicators works with the pairs (indicator, coefficient); in this case, we have two pairs (z[1], b[1]), (z[2], b[2]), and an indicator equal to 1 will trigger sampling of the corresponding coefficient.
>
> 2) Option B should be more efficient as it avoids the situation in 1, given that z[2] will be equal to 1 only if z[1] is equal to 1.
>
> Hope this helps!
>
> Sally
>
>
> On Mon, May 13, 2024 at 3:07 PM Kaitlin Macdonald <kaitlin.r...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Good Afternoon,
> >
> > I am trying to run a logistic regression using rjmcmc that includes a quadratic relationship between the explanatory and response variables. It seems that this should be quite simple, but I don't completely trust the way I have constructed the models. Based on what I've found in this group it looks like there are two ways to do this.
> >
> > Option A is to multiply the indicator for the linear term with the indicator for the quadratic term in the model which forces the quadratic to be zero if either indicator is estimated to be zero.
> > example: z[1]*b[1]*X1 + z[1]*z[2]*b[2]*X1^2
> >
> > Option B is to constrain the indicator for the quadratic term to be less than or equal to the indicator for the linear term.
> > example: constraint_data ~ dconstraint( z[2] <= z[1] )
> >
> > My questions are 1) are both options considered acceptable ways to include a quadratic relationship in the model? and 2) is one option more efficient than the other?
> >
> > I've attached screenshots with code for simulated examples of each option.
> >
> > Thanks for your help!
> >
> > Kaitlin
